<article>
  <p>Carding is the practice where police stop people to collect names and other personal data without an arrest or clear legal cause. The short clip that shows York Regional Police officers Konstantin Orshansky and Wu is a perfect example of how routine stops can slip into illegal detainment and a public relations mess that no training module can fix.</p>

  <h2>What carding actually costs communities</h2>
  <p>On paper carding sounds like helpful intelligence gathering. In reality it often produces noise, erodes community trust, and invites legal trouble. The evidence collected without reasonable grounds can be challenged in court and sometimes excluded from prosecutions. That is not theory, that is practical consequence for officers and departments that treat broad stops as a numbers game.</p>

  <h3>Four real problems with carding</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Legal exposure</strong> Police records made without reasonable grounds are at risk of being suppressed during trials, which means wasted effort and courtroom headaches.</li>
    <li><strong>Poor intelligence</strong> Random data lacks context. A list of names is not an investigation, it is noise until someone actually follows up on it.</li>
    <li><strong>Community harm</strong> People stopped for no clear reason lose trust in public safety efforts. That mistrust reduces cooperation and makes policing harder for everyone.</li>
    <li><strong>Resource waste</strong> Time spent on broad stops pulls officers away from targeted investigations that prevent crime.</li>
  </ul>
</article>